  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「カミガヤツリ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AB%E3%83%9F%E3%82%AC%E3%83%A4%E3%83%84%E3%83%AA",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106353268,
      "text": "カミガヤツリ は、カヤツリグサ科カヤツリグサ属の多年生の草本。パピルス（papyrus）、パピルス草、カミイ（紙葦）ともいい、茎の繊維がパピルスの原材料となる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Cyperus papyrus」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyperus_papyrus",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1365406091,
      "text": "Cyperus papyrus, better known by the common names papyrus sedge, papyrus, paper reed, Indian matting plant, or Nile grass, is a species of aquatic flowering plant belonging to the sedge family Cyperaceae. It is a tender herbaceous perennial, forming tall stands of reed-like swamp vegetation in shallow water."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Cyperus papyrus」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyperus_papyrus#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1365406091,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The papyrus plant is relatively easy to grow from seed, though in Egypt, it is more common to split the rootstock, and grows quite fast once established. Extremely moist soil or roots sunken in the water is preferred and the plant can flower all year long. Vegetative propagation is the suggested process of creating new plants. It is done by splitting the rhizomes into small groups and planting normally. C. papyrus is considered to be hardy in USDA hardiness zones 9 and 10.\nIn the United States, it has become invasive in Florida and has escaped from cultivation in Louisiana, California, and Hawaii.\nThe species as a whole, and the dwarf cultivar C. papyrus 'Nanus' have gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit (confirmed 2017).",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
